{
  "doc": [
    "One shared list of numeric spellings, swept by both runtimes' conformance suites over",
    "identical bytes. It is not a fixture and is deliberately not in `valid` or `invalid`:",
    "a valid fixture must re-encode to its own bytes, and `{\"protocol\":4.0}` re-encodes to",
    "`{\"protocol\":4}`, so the one class of case this list exists for cannot be one.",
    "",
    "To JSON Schema 2020-12 an `integer` is any number whose fractional part is zero, so a",
    "value is what is bounded here and a spelling never is. Each case carries the whole",
    "frame, so neither suite builds its own line; `accepted` is the schema's own verdict,",
    "checked by a real validator on the JVM side, and `value` is what `field` must decode",
    "to on both. A divergence therefore fails a build rather than waiting for a reviewer:",
    "the two runtimes' own lists of spellings had no literal in common where they",
    "disagreed, which is how two parity defects shipped inside the commits that were",
    "written to remove them.",
    "",
    "A literal amplifying *upward*, such as `1e2000000000`, is deliberately absent: it",
    "belongs to each runtime's own bounded tests, because a shared list is swept through",
    "the real validator too and comparing it against the schema's `maximum` would",
    "materialise the digits the guards exist to refuse. Its mirror *downward* is here,",
    "though, and is the one family this list must carry: a large negative exponent is the",
    "same cost through the other end of the same guard, it is what a one-sided bound let",
    "past, and the parser above rounds it to zero before any keyword sees it - so the",
    "validator prices it at nothing while a decoder that reads the literal has to refuse",
    "it. That asymmetry is exactly what a per-runtime list cannot state.",
    "",
    "Five cases carry `validatorAccepts` as well. A real validator reads these bytes",
    "through a parser that rounds a literal to a double before any keyword sees it, so",
    "`9007199254740991.4` and `1.0000000000000000001` reach it as whole numbers and it",
    "accepts them - which is exactly why neither binding trusts a parsed value and both",
    "read the literal off the frame instead. `accepted` stays the schema's own reading of",
    "the bytes, and so the verdict both decoders must reach; `validatorAccepts` records",
    "what the validator measurably does with them, so the JVM sweep states the limitation",
    "rather than being weakened by it."
  ],
  "cases": [
